  "query_preview": "..\n\nFog again developed overnight but not as widespread as last night \ndue to the southeasterly winds keeping the bulk of the Lake Huron \nmoisture out of the picture. Better moisture advection off Lake Erie \ntonight still resulted in dense fog downwind of the lake. Additional \nfog did develop across parts of the Thumb but more of the \nradiational variety as temps fell into the mid 30s which was enough \nwith the elevated dewpoints from residual lake moisture. So a Dense \nFog Advisory is in effect again this morning for two areas, the \nThumb and near the Ohio border. Fog should start mixing out around \n12-14Z again like the past two days. \n\nToday will once again feature high pressure at the surface, which is \nnow centered over New England. Meanwhile, a mid level ridge axis \nwill pass over the area today pulling the thermal ridge closer to \nthe area helping to increase highs a degree or two over yesterday, \nwhich will carry into Sunday where highs may then reach 70 across \nmuch of the area. Persistence forecasting with the high still \nholding on over the region this time of year suggests we could deal \nwith another night of fog but conditions are not as favorable with \nwarmer temps overnight and some high cloud starting to stream in. \n\nLongwave pattern then starts to break down to end the weekend with \nbase of the west coast trough cutting off and then lifting northeast \nthrough the Plains through the start of the new week. This occurs \nwhile a strong upper low tracks east across Canada helping to \nflatten the ridge across the Great Lakes. A cold front drops through \nthe state Monday which will bring cooler temps back briefly mid week \nbut a new wave coming ashore the Pacific NW looks to then reinforce \nthe west coast trough and in turn bulge the central conus ridge back \ninto the area bringing the warm air back. The overall pattern does \nremain flatter though with several mid level waves rippling through \nit after mid week which will bring some rain chances back to the \narea.",
